Falsifies `paging::x86_64::no_amd_vi_device_table_entry_sets_a_reserved_bit`.

The defect: `AmdVi::device_table_entry` masks its root into bits 51:12 instead of refusing a root
that does not fit, which is what the kernel's `translating_dte` did before provisional milestone 767
(AMD-Vi hardening before the first AMD boot). A root with a bit above 51, or an unaligned one, is
then silently turned into a different table's address, and the device walks that table.

Expected to fail: `assert!(fits, "a root the field cannot hold was accepted")`, on the first root
with a bit outside 51:12. The permitted-bits assertion does not see it, because the mask keeps the
entry inside the permitted bits; the read-back is what catches it, which is why the harness asks
both. Measured on patagonia 2026-10-05 (UTC), Kani 0.67.0.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs b/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
index 41683dfee..04f83bf85 100644
--- a/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
@@ -485,11 +485,8 @@ impl AmdVi {
     /// root field can hold, because the alternative is a silent mask that points the device at a
     /// different table. Name: provisional.
     pub const fn device_table_entry(root: u64, domain: u16) -> Option<[u64; 4]> {
-        if root & !AMD_VI_ADDR_MASK != 0 {
-            return None;
-        }
         Some([
-            DTE_V | DTE_TV | DTE_MODE_4_LEVEL | root | DTE_IR | DTE_IW,
+            DTE_V | DTE_TV | DTE_MODE_4_LEVEL | (root & AMD_VI_ADDR_MASK) | DTE_IR | DTE_IW,
             domain as u64,
             0,
             0,
